  "ai_language_tip": "Use polite Mongolian (Khalkha, Cyrillic) with 'ta'; English works at international hotels, some upscale Ulaanbaatar restaurants and tour operators; Russian is understood by many older staff but is not the default.",
  "english_ok": "sometimes — Common among younger staff in central Ulaanbaatar and tourism; rare at local shops, clinics and outside the capital.",
  "politeness": "Greet with 'Sain baina uu', use 'ta' (formal you), state you call on behalf of someone; close with 'bayarlalaa'.",
  "name_tip": "Mongolians use a given name with the father's name (ovog) as a patronymic initial rather than a family surname; bookings usually go under the given name — spell it.",
  "phone_tip": "Mobile 8 digits (prefixes such as 88, 99, 95, 96, 80, 85; e.g. 8812 3456), +976 country code; landlines rarely used by small businesses; Facebook Messenger is a common follow-up channel.",
  "time_tip": "24-hour in writing (19:30); spoken 'X tsag khagas' = X:30, '...minut dutuu' = minutes to the hour; day-part words (öglöö, ödör, oroi) mark AM/PM — read back as 24-hour.",
  "number_tip": "Western digits; mobile numbers are read in two groups of four (8812 3456) or in pairs; confirm digit by digit.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid the office lunch break (about 13:00–14:00), calls before 9:00 or after 21:00, and the national holidays of Tsagaan Sar (lunar new year) and Naadam (July) when many businesses close.",
  "closed_days": "Saturday–Sunday weekend for government and offices; shops and restaurants open daily; Tsagaan Sar (about 3 days, Jan/Feb) and Naadam (around 11–15 July; 10–15 July in 2026) close most offices and many businesses.",
  "booking_channels": "Taxis via UBCab and Toki; food via Toki, UBCab Eats and restaurant phone lines; government via e-Mongolia; shops and salons via Facebook pages and Messenger; payments via QPay, bank apps and Toki Pay.",
  "voice_menu": "Telecoms, banks and utilities use Mongolian IVR; restaurants, salons and small shops answer in person.",
  "callback_habits": "Businesses confirm by phone call or Messenger; delivery drivers call on arrival, often asking for the entrance and door number.",
